WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.732
A friend loves your restaurant, so they ask one waiter for a direct phone number.

2
00:00:07.732 --> 00:00:08.912
On Monday it works.

3
00:00:08.912 --> 00:00:11.272
On Wednesday the waiter changes shift.

4
00:00:11.272 --> 00:00:14.503
On Friday your friend calls, and a stranger answers.

5
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:20.649
The restaurant's own number would have worked every time, because it always reaches whoever is on the floor.

6
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:23.246
Pods have the same problem.

7
00:00:23.246 --> 00:00:28.187
Every Pod gets its own IP address, the number that names it on the network.

8
00:00:28.187 --> 00:00:30.361
Here are three hello-devops Pods.

9
00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:35.009
You copy one address from kubectl get pods into a settings file.

10
00:00:35.009 --> 00:00:37.300
It works today, so it feels safe.

11
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.480
That is the trap.

12
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.758
Then a rollout replaces that Pod.

13
00:00:40.758 --> 00:00:45.497
The new Pod gets a new address, and your settings file now points at nobody.

14
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.438
A Pod address in a settings file is the waiter's direct number.

15
00:00:49.438 --> 00:00:52.049
What we want is the restaurant's own number.

16
00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:56.062
Guests at a busy restaurant never hunt for one waiter.

17
00:00:56.062 --> 00:01:02.103
They go to the host stand at the door, and the host sends them to a waiter who is on duty and free.

18
00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:05.196
In Kubernetes, the host stand is a Service.

19
00:01:05.196 --> 00:01:09.591
It gives a changing group of Pods one fixed address, and one name.

20
00:01:09.591 --> 00:01:11.655
Here, hello-devops, on port 80.

21
00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:14.638
That fixed address is called the ClusterIP.

22
00:01:14.638 --> 00:01:16.957
It stays put while Pods come and go.

23
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:20.610
How does the Service know which waiters are its own?

24
00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.730
Not by name, since names change.

25
00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.643
By a label, a key and value tag on each Pod.

26
00:01:25.643 --> 00:01:28.756
Take every ready Pod labeled app: hello-devops.

27
00:01:29.567 --> 00:01:31.159
Now the host's clipboard.

28
00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:40.140
A controller watches the Pods and keeps a list, called an EndpointSlice: the address and port of every matching Pod, and whether it is ready.

29
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:41.610
Notice the port.

30
00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:44.485
Visitors call the Service on port 80.

31
00:01:44.485 --> 00:01:47.981
The app inside each Pod listens on port 8000.

32
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:56.994
And on every node, a program called kube-proxy reads both, and writes traffic rules into Linux, the operating system on the node.

33
00:01:56.994 --> 00:02:00.267
Traffic for the ClusterIP goes to one ready Pod.

34
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.034
So where does the host stand, exactly?

35
00:02:03.034 --> 00:02:04.034
Here is a test.

36
00:02:04.034 --> 00:02:09.036
You ping the ClusterIP, the command that checks whether an address answers.

37
00:02:09.036 --> 00:02:10.169
What answers you?

38
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:13.474
Nothing.

39
00:02:13.474 --> 00:02:16.273
The ClusterIP lives on no network card.

40
00:02:16.273 --> 00:02:18.426
It exists only as those rules.

41
00:02:18.426 --> 00:02:22.661
Here our picture bends: there is no host standing anywhere.

42
00:02:22.661 --> 00:02:25.819
Just very good signs, painted at every door.

43
00:02:26.433 --> 00:02:27.801
Now follow one call.

44
00:02:27.801 --> 00:02:31.631
A Pod sends a request to the Service's address, port 80.

45
00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:37.453
The rules on the caller's own node catch it, and pick one ready Pod from the clipboard.

46
00:02:37.453 --> 00:02:40.167
The packet goes to that Pod, on port 8000.

47
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.146
Only ready Pods get visitors.

48
00:02:42.146 --> 00:02:45.035
Say this Pod starts failing its readiness check.

49
00:02:45.035 --> 00:02:50.212
It keeps running, but the controller marks its entry not ready, and the rules skip it.

50
00:02:51.233 --> 00:02:53.703
Scale to five, and two new Pods appear.

51
00:02:53.703 --> 00:02:58.073
As soon as they are ready, the controller adds them to the clipboard.

52
00:02:58.073 --> 00:03:01.936
kubectl shows the first three addresses, and counts the rest.

53
00:03:01.936 --> 00:03:02.823
Plus two more.

54
00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:04.672
The floor changed, twice.

55
00:03:04.672 --> 00:03:08.145
The Service's name and address did not change at all.

56
00:03:08.967 --> 00:03:12.008
Now break it on purpose, the way the lab does.

57
00:03:12.008 --> 00:03:14.322
Change the selector to hello-devop.

58
00:03:14.322 --> 00:03:15.579
One letter missing.

59
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.590
No Pod carries that label, so the clipboard is empty.

60
00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:24.380
The Service still exists, and kubectl get svc looks perfectly fine.

61
00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:25.953
It just leads nowhere.

62
00:03:26.167 --> 00:03:29.789
A call arrives, and there is nobody to send it to.

63
00:03:29.789 --> 00:03:30.151
Well.

64
00:03:30.151 --> 00:03:34.208
That is a very polite host stand, with nobody behind it.

65
00:03:34.433 --> 00:03:35.748
So here is the rule.

66
00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:40.612
When a Service fails, read its EndpointSlice before you blame the network.

67
00:03:40.833 --> 00:03:44.697
No addresses there means a selector or a readiness problem.

68
00:03:44.697 --> 00:03:48.233
Addresses that refuse connections mean a port problem.

69
00:03:48.233 --> 00:03:51.115
Either way, it is rarely the network itself.

70
00:03:52.233 --> 00:03:56.315
The Service also has a name in the cluster's phone book, CoreDNS.

71
00:03:56.315 --> 00:04:01.527
Its full name is the Service, then the namespace, hello, then the cluster's ending.

72
00:04:01.733 --> 00:04:04.720
From the same namespace, the short name is enough.

73
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.229
From another namespace, add the namespace.

74
00:04:07.933 --> 00:04:09.526
One limit to keep in mind.

75
00:04:09.526 --> 00:04:12.527
The pick happens per connection, not per request.

76
00:04:12.527 --> 00:04:18.407
The kernel picks a Pod on a connection's first packet, and keeps it until the connection closes.

77
00:04:18.633 --> 00:04:27.082
So a client that keeps one connection open, like HTTP keep-alive or gRPC, can talk to the same Pod for a long time.

78
00:04:27.082 --> 00:04:31.489
A curl loop opens a new connection each time, so it spreads.

79
00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:33.912
The moral of the story.

80
00:04:33.912 --> 00:04:36.308
Pods change their numbers all the time.

81
00:04:36.308 --> 00:04:38.948
The Service keeps one name and one address.

82
00:04:38.948 --> 00:04:43.001
And its clipboard, the EndpointSlice, says who is ready right now.

83
00:04:43.233 --> 00:04:44.366
One quick check.

84
00:04:44.366 --> 00:04:49.248
curl gets no answer, and the EndpointSlice lists no addresses at all.

85
00:04:49.248 --> 00:04:50.805
Is the network broken?

86
00:04:53.533 --> 00:04:54.410
Almost never.

87
00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:57.176
An empty list means no ready Pod matches.

88
00:04:57.176 --> 00:05:01.425
Check the selector against the Pod labels, and check readiness.
